sec.p:
The total binding affinity and per-residue energy contribution between OTU protease and peptides were calculated using Molecular Mechanics/Poisson-Boltzmann Surface Area (MM/PBSA) and Molecular Mechanics/Generalized Born Surface Area (MM/GBSA) algorithms utilizing the g_mmpbsa tool of GROMACS-2023.4 software package34. The Python library, ParmEd35, was used to convert the pre-generated trajectory files from AMBER to GROMACS-compatible format for MM/PBSA and MM/GBSA calculations. For each complex, the last 10,000 frames (10 ns) were extracted from the simulation data and were subjected to binding free energy calculation. During MMPB/GBSA analysis, the three main components of the binding energy (molecular mechanical (MM), polar (ΔGpolar), and non-polar (ΔGnonpolar) solvation energy) were considered. Among these contributions, the MM energy comprises electrostatic (Eelec) and van der Waals (Evdw) energy. The mathematical expression used to calculate the total binding affinity between protein and peptides is as follows;\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\Delta G}_{binding}= {G}_{complex}-({G}_{protein} + {G}_{peptide})$$\end{document}

In this equation, the total binding free energy of the OTU-Peptide bound complexes was represented as Gcomplex. However, the Gprotein and Gpeptide are the total binding free energy of the isolated protein and peptides, respectively. Moreover, the vacuum potential energy (EMM) is based on the MM force field parameters. For the decomposition energy of each amino acid residue, the vacuum potential energy (ΔEMM), ΔGpolar, and ΔGnonpolar parameters were calculated separately. These parameters were then merged to obtain the energy contribution of each amino acid residue (x) to the binding energy (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
